**Read:** [Lectures on Non-Newtonian Fluid Dynamics — John Hinch](../sources/Non-Newtonian-Fluid-Dynamics-Hinch.lingua#r_06KY7WE9WYAF856YQKQXHY1DS3), rheometry and experimental methods; [Macromolecular Hydrodynamics — Armstrong and McKinley](../sources/Macromolecular-Hydrodynamics-MIT-2-341J.lingua#r_4VRCY0V06HMS11NTC3XHS84QQB), topic 2 material functions and available assignments; [Experimental Methods in Fluid Mechanics — Pranab K. Mondal](../sources/Experimental-Methods-in-Fluids-Mondal.lingua#r_4XFYEGQVB1EV4QF9BP17ME04Y4), measurement-system response and uncertainty.

A rheometer measures forces, torque, displacement and time through a particular geometry. Turning those readings into stress and strain rate requires assumptions about the flow, wall condition and sample. Derive the relation for one ideal narrow-gap or cone-and-plate arrangement, then identify finite-gap, edge and inertia corrections from the selected method.

For capillary rheometry, separate entrance pressure loss, fully developed shear, non-Newtonian profile correction and possible wall slip. For oscillatory measurements, assess instrument inertia, compliance and usable amplitude/frequency ranges. Extensional measurements have their own kinematic uniformity and free-surface difficulties; a tensile force divided by one area is not automatically a homogeneous material function.

Build an uncertainty account for a specific experiment and compare overlapping measurements from two geometries or protocols. Preserve temperature control, loading, rest history, evaporation and sample changes. Demonstrate the range in which the inferred property is repeatable and model-independent enough for the intended flow prediction.

